  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The weight, in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth grows at a rate of $3{t}^{2}+15.$ If, at $t=1$ years old, the gorilla weighed $20$ pounds, how much will the gorilla weigh at $3$ years old?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $s(t)=3{t}^{2}+15$ be the rate at which the weight of the gorilla is changing and let $S(T)$ be the weight of the gorilla $T$ years after birth. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+{\\int }_{1}^{T}3{t}^{2}+15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+({t}^{3}+15t){|}_{1}^{T} \\\\  & =20+({T}^{3}+15T)-({1}^{3}+15\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20+{T}^{3}+15T-16 \\\\  & ={T}^{3}+15T+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the weight of the gorilla $T=3$ years after birth, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(3)={3}^{3}+15\\cdot 3+4=76.\n$$",
